Chapter: PlugOracleArith

 Integer division and the two remainders are what this asks about, because
 that is where languages disagree silently rather than failing to compile.
 Codex `/` truncates toward zero, `int-rem` takes the sign of the dividend,
 and `int-mod` is Euclidean. Python, Ruby, Lua and Perl floor instead;
 Haskell's `div` floors where its `quot` truncates; Dart's `%` is never
 negative. Every one of those disagreements is invisible to a compile and
 invisible to a round trip through the same plug.

Section: Operations

  dv : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dv (a) (b) = a / b

  rm : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  rm (a) (b) = int-rem a b

  md : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  md (a) (b) = int-mod a b

Section: Bounded fields

 A clamping bounded field saturates at the declared band rather than
 wrapping or trapping. No target language does this natively: C#, Python
 and JavaScript all store what they are given, so the saturation has to
 be emitted by the plug from the field's declared type. That makes it the
 same class of silent disagreement as the remainders above -- a store
 that stops clamping compiles, runs, and answers 150 where the answer is
 100.

  Gauge = record {
    g : Integer between -100 and 100 clamping
  }

  gauge : Integer -> Integer
  gauge (n) = (Gauge { g = n }).g

Section: List literals

 A statically typed target has to name the element type of a list before
 it can build one, and a literal is the one place that type appears
 nowhere but in the elements. Zig rejects the array outright when the
 plug guesses wrong; C and Java would take a wrong guess and run. The
 length and the element read are both here because a wrong element type
 can still produce a list of the right length.

  lit-len : Integer -> Integer
  lit-len (n) = list-length [10, 20, 30, 40]

  lit-at : Integer -> Integer
  lit-at (i) = list-at [10, 20, 30, 40] i

Section: List push

 `list-push` and `list-snoc` are two SOURCE names for one operation and the
 IR carries whichever the source wrote, so a plug that registers only one of
 them emits a call to a function it never defines for the other. Sixteen
 registered `list-snoc` alone while `list-push` is the spelling this codebase
 uses, and nothing here saw it because this subject exercised neither name.

  push-len : Integer -> Integer
  push-len (n) = list-length (list-push [10, 20] n)

  push-at : Integer -> Integer
  push-at (n) = list-at (list-push [10, 20] n) 2

  snoc-len : Integer -> Integer
  snoc-len (n) = list-length (list-snoc [10, 20] n)

 Aliasing is deliberately NOT asked about here. Measured on x86-64
 2026-08-16: after `let a = [] in let b = list-push a n in let c =
 list-push a (n + 1)`, all three names report length 2 and the same element,
 so `__list_snoc` extended the topmost allocation in place and returned the
 same pointer both times. A non-empty literal behaves the same way. No
 transpilation target reproduces that, and a row asserting it would fail every
 plug that copies -- which is most of them -- while passing the ones that
 append in place. It belongs in plugs-backlog 1.7, not in a cross-language
 oracle.

Section: Mutable records

 A store into a `mutable` record field is the quietest thing a plug can get
 wrong: emit it as an expression whose result is discarded and the program
 compiles, runs, and answers the value the field had BEFORE the store. Kotlin
 does exactly that (`rec.copy(field = value)` in statement position) and Scala
 has the same shape, so this is not hypothetical. Nothing ran a plug's output
 on a mutable record before this.

 Two fields and two stores, because a plug can emit a single store correctly by
 accident and still drop the second, and the read-back is what makes a
 discarded store visible at all.

  mutable Cell = record {
    ca : Integer,
    cb : Integer
  }

  store-one : Integer -> Integer
  store-one (n) =
    let c = Cell { ca = 0, cb = 0 }
    in c.ca = n
    in c.ca

  store-two : Integer -> Integer
  store-two (n) =
    let c = Cell { ca = 0, cb = 0 }
    in c.ca = n
    in c.cb = c.ca + 1
    in c.cb

  store-untouched : Integer -> Integer
  store-untouched (n) =
    let c = Cell { ca = 0, cb = 7 }
    in c.ca = n
    in c.cb

Section: Deep recursion

 Codex source assumes deep recursion is free: bare metal answers it with a
 multi-gigabyte arena. A conventional runtime answers it with a fixed stack or
 a counter, and the case that reaches the limit is MUTUAL recursion, which no
 self-tail-call pass can flatten. Both shapes are here because a self-only row
 is green on every plug that has TCO and says nothing.

  down : Integer -> Integer
  down (n) = if n <= 0 then 0 else down (n - 1)

  ping : Integer -> Integer
  ping (n) = if n <= 0 then 0 else pong (n - 1)

  pong : Integer -> Integer
  pong (n) = if n <= 0 then 1 else ping (n - 1)

 sum-to is the row nothing can flatten, because the call is not in tail
 position, and the only recursion row whose answer is a large number rather
 than 0: a plug that returns a wrong value instead of dying is caught here.

  sum-to : Integer -> Integer
  sum-to (n) = if n <= 0 then 0 else n + sum-to (n - 1)

Section: Lambdas

 The wire does NOT lift lambdas, so a plug meets capture itself: a lambda
 that reads an enclosing local arrives with that local as a bare name and
 no marker. Six plugs carry their own lifting and the rest lean on the
 target language's closures, which is correct wherever the target has
 them. Fortran and C89 have none, so those emit a refusal rather than a
 body, and that is the declared behaviour rather than a gap.

 A lambda of more than one parameter arrives as nested application, one
 argument at a time. A target that spells this as a single call with all
 the arguments and a target that spells it as a chain of one-argument
 calls both run, and they disagree only when the chain is applied to more
 arguments than the innermost lambda declares.

 The second parameter carries a DIFFERENT type from the first on purpose.
 Every parameter here typed Integer would let a wrong or missing type on
 any parameter but the first run correctly anyway, which is exactly how
 this class stayed invisible: a lambda whose later parameters reached the
 wire untyped answered correctly on x86-64, which needs no annotation to
 build the call, and would be refused by a statically typed target and
 guessed at by C or Java.

  lam-one : Integer -> Integer
  lam-one (n) = (\x -> x + 100) n

  lam-two : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  lam-two (a) (b) = ((\x y -> x + y) a) b

  lam-three : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  lam-three (a) (b) (c) = (((\x y z -> x + y * z) a) b) c

  lam-mixed : Integer, Boolean -> Integer
  lam-mixed (n) (f) = ((\x b -> if b then x else 0 - x) n) f

  lam-capture : Integer -> Integer
  lam-capture (n) = let k = 10 in (\x -> x + k) n

  lam-capture-two : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  lam-capture-two (a) (b) = let k = 100 in ((\x y -> (x + y) * k) a) b

Section: Match guards

 A guarded arm fires only when its pattern matches AND its guard holds;
 when the guard refuses, the match falls through to the next arm. A plug
 that lowers a match to the target's switch and never reads the guard
 emits a program that compiles clean on both sides and answers
 differently, which is the worst shape: no refusal, a wrong value. The
 rows are chosen so the difference is a wrong answer rather than a compile
 error: two arms on the same constructor with different guards, a guard on
 a catch-all, and a guarded arm whose payload is a tuple.

  Val = | Num (Integer) | Pair (Integer) (Integer) | Nil

  classify : Val -> Integer
  classify (v) =
   when v
    is Num (n) when n < 0 -> 1
    is Num (n) when n > 100 -> 2
    is Num (n) -> 3
    is Pair (a) (b) when a == b -> 4
    is Pair (a) (b) -> a - b
    is otherwise -> 0

  band : Integer -> Integer
  band (n) =
   when n
    is 0 -> 0
    is x when x < 0 -> 1
    is otherwise -> 2

Section: Report

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
   print-line-uni (show (dv 7 3))
   print-line-uni (show (dv (0 - 7) 3))
   print-line-uni (show (dv 7 (0 - 3)))
   print-line-uni (show (dv (0 - 7) (0 - 3)))
   print-line-uni (show (dv (0 - 9) 8))
   print-line-uni (show (dv (0 - 8) 8))
   print-line-uni (show (rm 7 3))
   print-line-uni (show (rm (0 - 7) 3))
   print-line-uni (show (rm 7 (0 - 3)))
   print-line-uni (show (rm (0 - 7) (0 - 3)))
   print-line-uni (show (rm (0 - 9) 8))
   print-line-uni (show (rm (0 - 8) 8))
   print-line-uni (show (md 7 3))
   print-line-uni (show (md (0 - 7) 3))
   print-line-uni (show (md 7 (0 - 3)))
   print-line-uni (show (md (0 - 7) (0 - 3)))
   print-line-uni (show (md (0 - 9) 8))
   print-line-uni (show (gauge 150))
   print-line-uni (show (gauge (0 - 150)))
   print-line-uni (show (gauge 42))
   print-line-uni (show (lit-len 0))
   print-line-uni (show (lit-at 2))
   print-line-uni (show (push-len 30))
   print-line-uni (show (push-at 30))
   print-line-uni (show (snoc-len 30))
   print-line-uni (show (store-one 55))
   print-line-uni (show (store-two 55))
   print-line-uni (show (store-untouched 55))
   print-line-uni (show (down 100000))
   print-line-uni (show (ping 1000))
   print-line-uni (show (ping 100000))
   print-line-uni (show (sum-to 1000))
   print-line-uni (show (sum-to 100000))
   print-line-uni (show (lam-one 5))
   print-line-uni (show (lam-two 1 2))
   print-line-uni (show (lam-three 2 3 4))
   print-line-uni (show (lam-mixed 7 True))
   print-line-uni (show (lam-mixed 7 False))
   print-line-uni (show (lam-capture 5))
   print-line-uni (show (lam-capture-two 1 2))
   print-line-uni (show (classify (Num 5)))
   print-line-uni (show (classify (Num (0 - 5))))
   print-line-uni (show (classify (Num 500)))
   print-line-uni (show (classify (Pair 7 7)))
   print-line-uni (show (classify (Pair 9 2)))
   print-line-uni (show (classify Nil))
   print-line-uni (show (band 0))
   print-line-uni (show (band (0 - 4)))
   print-line-uni (show (band 4))
  end
